<!----></response-element><h3 data-path-to-node=3D"9">2. The Transfer Logic=
 (Service Layer)</h3><p data-path-to-node=3D"10">When you move furniture fr=
om Troop A to Troop B, you don't just flip a flag; you perform a <b data-pa=
th-to-node=3D"10" data-index-in-node=3D"91">Split and Reassign</b> operatio=
n. This keeps your data "clean" and standard JPA queries will just work.</p=
><ul data-path-to-node=3D"11"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"11,0,0"><b data-p=
ath-to-node=3D"11,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">If moving the WHOLE batch:<=
/b> Just change the <code data-path-to-node=3D"11,0,0" data-index-in-node=
=3D"43">owner</code> field to the new Troop.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-no=
de=3D"11,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"11,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">If m=
oving PART of a batch:</b> Decrease the <code data-path-to-node=3D"11,1,0" =
data-index-in-node=3D"40">amount</code> on the original row and create a <b=
 data-path-to-node=3D"11,1,0" data-index-in-node=3D"80">new row</b> for the=
 receiving Troop.</p></li></ul><hr data-path-to-node=3D"12"><h2 data-path-t=
o-node=3D"13">Why this is better than your "Flag" solution:</h2><ol start=
=3D"1" data-path-to-node=3D"14"><li><p data-path-to-node=3D"14,0,0"><b data=
-path-to-node=3D"14,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"0">Standard JPA Fetching:</b=
> You don't need custom queries to "find moved items." You just call <code =
data-path-to-node=3D"14,0,0" data-index-in-node=3D"90">troop.getFurnitures(=
)</code> and JPA returns everything currently owned by that troop.</p></li>=
<li><p data-path-to-node=3D"14,1,0"><b data-path-to-node=3D"14,1,0" data-in=
